<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="ad3b40cf" lines="72-87">
<description>
Mirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.
- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.

Rule
- Preserve the input grid.
- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.
- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.

Key insight
- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="edcc2ff0" lines="47-63">
<description>
Keep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.

Input structure
A black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers
in the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has
a unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.

Rule
In the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground
object to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0
whose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.

Key insight
The top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects
survive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="5792cb4d" lines="44-59">
<description>
Reverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.
- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.

Rule:
- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.
- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.
- Leave every background pixel unchanged.

Key insight:
- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="458e3a53" lines="19-32">
<description>
Extract the compact table of solid cells hidden inside a separator lattice.

Input structure
- The grid has full-width and full-height bars of one separator color, dividing it into rectangular cells.
- Most cells contain a repeating multicolor pattern, while a contiguous rectangle of cells is solid monochrome.

Rule
- Split the input into lattice cells using the full separator rows and columns.
- Keep the cells whose full interior is one color, crop to their occupied rectangle, and output those colors.

Key insight
- The periodic pattern is noise; only completely solid lattice cells encode the reduced output grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="1a244afd" lines="49-68">
<description>
Rotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.
- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same
  row or column.

Rule:
- Remove all magenta source pixels.
- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the
  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.
- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the
  grid, the source simply disappears.

Key insight:
The blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a
unique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="d968ffd4" lines="15-28">
<description>
Extend each of two aligned rectangular marker objects toward the other, filling half of the gap across the full perpendicular span.

Input structure
- A dominant background contains exactly two solid, same-sized monochromatic rectangles.
- The rectangles share either the same row span or the same column span, with a background gap between their facing edges.

Rule
- If the rectangles are side-by-side, color the left half of the inter-object gap across every row with the left object's color, and the right half with the right object's color.
- If one rectangle is above the other, color the upper half of the gap across every column with the upper object's color, and the lower half with the lower object's color.
- When the gap length is odd, leave the center row/column as background.

Key insight
- The marker objects are endpoints of an axial corridor; their own size identifies the facing edge, while the filled corridor spans the whole grid perpendicular to that axis.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="b0039139" lines="29-44">
<description>
Recolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.

Input structure:
- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.
- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.
- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.

Rule:
- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.
- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.
- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.

Key insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation
is inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="b7955b3c" lines="386-403">
<description>
Replace gray plus-shaped damage masks with the hidden colors of the rectilinear mosaic.

Input structure:
- Color 8 appears only as isolated 3x3 plus masks.
- The non-mask pixels are visible parts of a clean grid made from horizontal
  and vertical color regions.

Rule:
- Treat every color-8 pixel as unknown damage and leave all other pixels fixed.
- First copy unknown pixels from exact matching parallel rows or columns.
- Then fill any remaining mask cells from opposite-side continuations and the
  better-supported local straight edge.

Key insight:
- The gray pluses are occluders, not objects; the underlying colors are
  determined by repeated line templates and nearby axis-aligned boundaries.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="de809cff" lines="86-103">
<description>
Turn black dents in two colored regions into marked opposite-color patches.

Input structure:
- Black background with two large foreground colors.
- The large regions contain small black dents and a few one-cell color specks
  or protrusions along region boundaries.

Rule:
- Locally clean the two foreground regions: isolated specks become black, and
  one-cell protrusions surrounded by the other region take that region's color.
- Each original black dent whose cleaned 3x3 neighborhood is mostly one
  foreground color becomes the center of a 3x3 patch in the opposite color.
- The dent center itself is recolored teal (8).

Key insight: The teal pixels mark the original black defects; the surrounding
3x3 patch uses the other foreground color, after first removing boundary noise.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="136b0064" lines="25-39">
<description>
Draw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.

Input structure:
- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.
- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.

Rule:
- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.
- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).
- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.
- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.

Key insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="ac6f9922" lines="18-40">
<description>
Summarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.

Input structure
- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot
  backgrounds.
- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of
  slots, ordered by their positions.
- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.

Rule
- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch
  the input edge.
- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output
  table.
- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise
  output the background color.

Key insight
- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are
  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary
  value.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="df9fd884" lines="33-47">
<description>
Move the colored object from the lower bracket lane to the upper bracket lane and drop it to the bottom edge.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- One frame color forms exactly two 4-connected bracket objects: a top-edge bracket open downward and a bottom-edge bracket open upward.
- One other 4-connected foreground object sits in the lower bracket's interior column span.

Rule
- Remove the movable object from its original cells.
- Keep its shape and color unchanged.
- Paste it with its bbox columns aligned to the top bracket's interior columns and with its bbox bottom aligned to the grid bottom.

Key insight
- The lower bracket identifies the object's source lane; the upper bracket provides the target lane, and the final vertical placement is bottom-aligned.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="train" puzzle_id="d6542281" lines="72-91">
<description>
Complete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.

Input structure
- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.
- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.
- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.

Rule
- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a
  same-color sprite pixel.
- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,
  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.
- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as
  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.

Key insight
The scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite
determines where the full sprite must be completed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="train" puzzle_id="c9680e90" lines="52-67">
<description>
Move each mirrored bottom source pixel to the far end of its trail and redraw the top half as its reflection.

Input structure
- A solid non-background row separates equal-height top and bottom halves.
- The top half has one marker color on the background.
- In the bottom half, exactly one color has positions that mirror the top markers across the separator, and the other color forms trails touching those source pixels.

Rule
- Identify the bottom source color by reflecting each bottom color into the top half and matching the top marker set.
- For every bottom source pixel, follow its unique 4-connected trail of the other bottom color to the trail endpoint.
- Clear the old top markers, bottom sources, and trails; place the source color at each endpoint and place the top marker color at the reflected endpoint.

Key insight
- The top half is only a mirror display of the bottom moving sources; the actual motion is encoded by the trail attached to each bottom source pixel.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="15" source="train" puzzle_id="902510d5" lines="58-80">
<description>
Keep the largest 8-connected motif and replace the corner/control pixels with a solid corner triangle.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Exactly one grid corner contains a foreground control pixel; it gives the
  corner from which the triangle is painted.
- There is one unique largest 8-connected monochrome foreground object, the
  motif that remains in the output; all other foreground pixels are controls.
- Every foreground component outside that motif is a singleton control pixel.
- The fill color is the unique control color that appears in at least two
  non-corner control pixels.

Rule
- Start from a blank background canvas and redraw only the largest motif.
- Count the non-corner control pixels; that count is the triangle side.
- Paint the fill color on the right-triangle region where those two distances
  from the marked corner's edges sum to less than the side length.

Key insight
- The scattered singleton controls are counted into a compact triangular
  stack at the marked corner; only the largest motif is not part of the count.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="16" source="train" puzzle_id="252143c9" lines="14-33">
<description>
Keep only the center color and redraw it as a diagonal ray to its indicated corner.

Input structure:
- The grid is an odd square with a unique dominant background color.
- The center pixel is foreground.
- Every off-center pixel with the center color lies in the same diagonal
  quadrant relative to the center.

Rule:
- Fill the whole output with the background color.
- Use the quadrant of the center-colored pixels to choose one diagonal
  direction from the center.
- Draw a continuous line of the center color from the center to the grid
  edge in that direction.

Key insight:
The color at the exact center identifies the surviving color, while the
other pixels of that color identify which corner the center should connect to.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="17" source="train" puzzle_id="8886d717" lines="43-63">
<description>
Repair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.

Input structure:
- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.
- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object
  color and a smaller marker color.
- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at
  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.

Rule:
- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.
- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent
  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.
- All other pixels are unchanged.

Key insight:
The full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background
markers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply
defects to be filled.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="18" source="train" puzzle_id="5adee1b2" lines="8-23">
<description>
Use the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.

Input structure:
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.
- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.

Rule:
- Keep the input pixels unchanged.
- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.
- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.

Key insight:
- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="19" source="train" puzzle_id="182e5d0f" lines="89-103">
<description>
Move each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.
- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.

Rule:
- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.
- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.
- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.

Key insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="20" source="train" puzzle_id="996ec1f3" lines="16-31">
<description>
Compress the noisy four-region board split by a solid cross into a 3x3 color map.

Input structure:
- A single full row and a single full column have the same solid separator color.
- The separator cross divides the input into four nonempty rectangular regions.
- Each region has a unique dominant color, with other colors acting as noise.

Rule:
- Find the unique solid same-color row/column pair.
- Fill the middle row and middle column of the 3x3 output with the separator color.
- Put the unique dominant color of each quadrant into the corresponding corner.

Key insight:
- The input grid is a noisy enlarged version of the desired 3x3 layout; the cross marks the coarse cell boundaries.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

